Step 1: Set up the RDS Database (1:37 – 4:52)

Navigate to the RDS Dashboard and create a new database.
Select MySQL (version 8.4.5) with a db.t3.micro instance type.
Configure the storage to 5GB using the gp2 storage type.
Crucial: Set the database to be private (Public access: No) to ensure security.
Set a database identifier, master username, and initial database name (DevOpsDB).
Step 2: Configure Security Groups (5:19 – 8:42)

Delete older sg in rd db and create a newone, also in ec2 create a new sg change the allocation of sg of the ec2 to new sg.
Create a security group for the EC2 instance that allows SSH (port 22) and HTTP (port 80) traffic.
Create a separate security group for the RDS instance.
Modify the RDS inbound rules to allow traffic only on port 3306 (MySQL) originating from the security group assigned to your EC2 instance.
Step 3: Establish Passwordless Authentication (9:00 – 12:12)

Generate SSH keys on the local client machine if they do not exist.
Connect to the EC2 instance and add the public key to the ~/.ssh/authorized_keys file to enable secure, passwordless access.
Step 4: Deploy the Application (12:16 – 16:51)

Use SCP to copy your index.php file from the local client to the /var/www/html directory on the EC2 instance.
Log in to the EC2 instance and verify the file transfer.
Edit the index.php file to include the specific database credentials (endpoint/host, username, password, and database name).
Finally, verify the setup by navigating to the public IP of the EC2 instance in a web browser to confirm a "Connection Successful" message.
